const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is POMC deficiency?',
    a: 'POMC deficiency is a rare genetic condition in which the body cannot make proopiomelanocortin (POMC), the precursor protein that is normally cut into several melanocortin hormones. Without it, children develop severe obesity from infancy, adrenal insufficiency, and — because the same precursor feeds the pigment pathway — characteristically red hair and pale skin.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does POMC deficiency cause red hair?',
    a: 'Because α-MSH, the pigment signal that switches on MC1R, is one of the peptides cut from POMC. No POMC means no α-MSH, so MC1R is never told to make dark eumelanin — the same end result as an inactive MC1R gene in a natural redhead. It is a striking visible clue that one broken gene is behind the obesity, the pale skin, and the hormone problem at once.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How is it treated?',
    a: 'Setmelanotide (Imcivree), an MC4R agonist, re-supplies the appetite signal the missing α-MSH should have provided, and in trials most people with POMC deficiency lost substantial weight. It does not replace the other missing hormones — the adrenal insufficiency still needs lifelong steroid replacement, managed separately by an endocrinologist.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is LEPR deficiency the same thing?',
    a: 'It is the neighbouring break in the same circuit. Leptin-receptor (LEPR) deficiency sits one step upstream: the POMC neurons are intact but never hear leptin’s signal to fire. The downstream result — unrelenting hunger and early, severe obesity — is similar, and setmelanotide is approved for both because it acts below the break, at MC4R.',
  },
];

