<PageLayout
  title="POMC & leptin-receptor deficiency: when the appetite signal is missing from birth"
  kicker="The condition"
  description="POMC deficiency is the rare genetic disease that proved the melanocortin appetite circuit is real in humans — a single missing precursor that causes severe childhood obesity, adrenal failure, and red hair at once. It was also the first condition setmelanotide was approved to treat."
  thread="One precursor gone, and three systems fall silent together — the clearest proof that a single melanocortin gene can hold colour, hormones, and hunger in one hand."
  updated="2026"
  about={[
    { name: 'Proopiomelanocortin deficiency', type: 'MedicalCondition', sameAs: ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proopiomelanocortin'] },
    { name: 'Melanocortin 4 receptor', type: 'MedicalEntity', sameAs: ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melanocortin_4_receptor'] },
    { name: 'Setmelanotide', type: 'Drug', sameAs: ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Setmelanotide'] },
  ]}
>
  <KeyTakeaways
    items={[
      'POMC deficiency is a rare genetic disease in which the melanocortin precursor POMC is absent — so every peptide normally cut from it goes missing at once.',
      'The result is a striking triad: severe early-onset obesity (no α-MSH at MC4R), adrenal insufficiency (no ACTH), and red hair with pale skin (no α-MSH at MC1R).',
      'It is the clearest natural proof that the leptin–melanocortin appetite circuit is real in humans: break the signal at its source and hunger becomes relentless from infancy.',
      'Leptin-receptor (LEPR) deficiency is the neighbouring break — POMC neurons intact but deaf to leptin — with the same downstream hunger, which is why one drug treats both.',
      'Setmelanotide (Imcivree) re-supplies the missing appetite signal at MC4R and was first approved for exactly these conditions in 2020 — but it replaces only that arm, not the adrenal one.',
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    If you wanted a single disease to prove that the <a href="/receptors/mc4r">melanocortin
    appetite circuit</a> is not a mouse curiosity but a real human control system, this is
    it. <strong>POMC deficiency</strong> is what happens when the body cannot make{" "}
    <a href="/system">proopiomelanocortin</a> — the precursor protein that is supposed to be
    cut, tissue by tissue, into the melanocortin hormones. Remove the precursor and you do
    not lose one signal; you lose all of them at once, and the result is a child whose
    colouring, hormones, and appetite all point back to the same broken gene.<Cite n={3} />
  </p>

  <h2>One missing precursor, three failures</h2>
  <p>
    The hallmark of POMC deficiency is a triad that looks, at first, like three unrelated
    problems. There is <strong>adrenal insufficiency</strong>, because{" "}
    <a href="/acth">ACTH</a> — the POMC peptide that drives the adrenal glands — is missing,
    and this is the dangerous part, present from the newborn period. There is
    <strong>red hair and pale skin</strong>, because <a href="/alpha-msh">α-MSH</a>, the
    pigment signal, is also cut from POMC: with none of it reaching{" "}
    <a href="/receptors/mc1r">MC1R</a>, the pigment switch is never flipped to dark, exactly
    as in a natural redhead with an inactive MC1R gene. And there is <strong>severe,
    early-onset obesity</strong> with relentless hunger, because the same α-MSH is missing at{" "}
    <a href="/receptors/mc4r">MC4R</a>, the brain's appetite brake.<Cite n={3} />
  </p>
  <p>
    That the three travel together is the whole point. It is one of biology's cleanest
    demonstrations that a single precursor is reused across colour, the stress axis, and
    energy balance — the thesis this entire site is built on, written in a patient.
  </p>

  <Figure
    caption="Why one gene causes three problems: with POMC absent, every peptide cut from it goes missing. ACTH is gone (adrenal insufficiency); α-MSH is gone at MC1R (red hair, pale skin) and at MC4R (severe obesity). Setmelanotide re-supplies the MC4R arm only — which is why the adrenal insufficiency still needs separate hormone replacement."
    source="Clinical triad and setmelanotide mechanism per StatPearls NBK589641 and Clément et al., Lancet Diabetes Endocrinol 2020 (PMID 33137293). Schematic."
  >
    <PomcTriad />
  </Figure>

  <h2>LEPR deficiency: the neighbouring break</h2>
  <p>
    POMC deficiency rarely travels alone in the clinic's thinking, because it has a close
    neighbour. In <strong>leptin-receptor (LEPR) deficiency</strong>, the POMC neurons
    themselves are intact — but they never hear the signal to fire. Leptin, the hormone fat
    releases to report how full the body's energy stores are, arrives at the arcuate nucleus
    and finds no working receptor to bind. The POMC neurons stay quiet, α-MSH is not
    released, and the downstream result is the same picture of unrelenting hunger and severe,
    early obesity.<Cite n={3} /> One disease removes the precursor; the other removes the
    switch that should turn the precursor's neurons on. Both end at the same silent MC4R.
  </p>

  <h2>Why one drug treats both</h2>
  <p>
    This shared endpoint is exactly why a single drug works for both conditions.{" "}
    <a href="/setmelanotide">Setmelanotide</a> is an MC4R agonist: it acts <em>below</em> the
    break, re-supplying the appetite signal that the missing α-MSH — whether absent because
    POMC is gone or because the leptin signal never arrived — should have carried. In the
    pivotal Phase 3 trials, about 80% of participants with POMC deficiency and 45% with LEPR
    deficiency achieved at least 10% weight loss at roughly a year, alongside large
    reductions in hunger.<Cite n={1} /> On that basis the FDA approved setmelanotide in
    <strong>November 2020</strong> for obesity due to POMC, PCSK1, or LEPR deficiency — its
    first approval, and the first drug ever cleared for these diseases.<Cite n={2} />
  </p>
  <p>
    (PCSK1 deficiency belongs in the same group for the same reason: PCSK1 encodes one of the
    enzymes that actually <em>cuts</em> POMC into its active peptides, so losing it starves the
    pathway of α-MSH just as losing POMC itself does.)<Cite n={3} />
  </p>

  <h2>The honest limits</h2>
  <p>
    Setmelanotide addresses the appetite arm, and only that arm. The adrenal insufficiency of
    POMC deficiency is not touched by it and still requires lifelong steroid replacement,
    managed by an endocrinologist — a reminder that re-supplying one melanocortin signal does
    not rebuild all the others the missing precursor should have provided.<Cite n={3} /> The
    drug also carries the melanocortin class's built-in effects, including skin darkening from
    its action at MC1R and nausea. It is a genuine, disease-specific treatment for a condition
    that previously had none — not a cure for the underlying genetic defect.
  </p>

  <h2>The honest bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    POMC and LEPR deficiency are rare, but they carry outsized weight in this field: they are
    the experiments of nature that showed the leptin–melanocortin circuit really governs human
    appetite, and they are where setmelanotide — now used far more broadly — first proved
    itself. For the drug, see <a href="/setmelanotide">setmelanotide</a>; for the receptor it
    targets, see <a href="/receptors/mc4r">MC4R</a>; for the wider catalogue of genes that can
    break this pathway, see <a href="/genetics">the genetics of the system</a>; and for the
    acquired version of the same problem, see <a href="/hypothalamic-obesity">hypothalamic
    obesity</a>.
  </p>

